UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 2: Drafting, editing and publishing a narrative', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite a flat ending in two sentences that show resolution through action.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.\n\nCHECK\nCalling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nFinish a short narrative through draft→edit→publish. Submit a clean copy plus a five-item edit log.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN4-ECA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Finish a short narrative through draft→edit→publish. Submit a clean copy plus a five-item edit log. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''. Reasoning: I used draft by attending to edit rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Correction: Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''. Why: the correction restores accurate use of draft.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating draft on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with draft, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''. This matches the lesson skill for draft.","options":["Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.","Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Rewrite a flat ending in two sentences that show resolution through action. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not…","Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.","Careful use of edit with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising draft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named edit, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the edit used."],"question":"A learner is practising draft. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries draft.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words.","Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show draft and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses draft from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.","Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about draft?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise edit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess.","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about draft.","Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of draft to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating draft on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''. Reasoning: I used draft by attending to edit rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with draft, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Correction: Strong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''. Why: the correction restores accurate use of draft.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w04-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise draft.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use draft with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify edit and explain why it matters.","I can practise publish independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings. A supported explanation follows the…","options":["Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about draft?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDraft ending: ''Then everything was fine.'' Edited: ''Mara pinned the last label as the first visitor’s shoes clicked on the concrete.'' Publish checklist: consistent POV, paragraphing, title, name.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite a flat ending in two sentences that show resolution through action.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.","explain":"Publishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Publish path.","caption":"Then clean copy.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Draft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">events down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit</t