erline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Ending through action.","caption":"Show resolution.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Flat end</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">all fine</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shown end</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">pins last label</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no explanation.","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about draft?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Here is a more productive model: Draft ending: ''Then everything was fine.'' Edited: ''Mara pinned the last label as the first visitor’s shoes clicked on the concrete.'' Publish checklist: consistent POV, paragraphing, title, name.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Shows the goal met or changed through visible action; avoids empty ''fine/happy''.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Calling a first draft finished because the plot exists. Audience-ready work needs editing passes. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about draft?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPublishing a narrative moves from draft (get events down) to edit (clarity, viewpoint, pacing) to a clean published version for an audience. Editing is not only spelling; it includes cutting repetition and strengthening openings and endings. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new content. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Transfer to an independent example"}],"title":"Year 7, Week 4, Lesson 2: Drafting, editing and publishing a narrative","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: short stories and voice","week":4,"week_in_unit":4,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nDraft ending: ''Then everything was fine.'' Edited: ''Mara pinned the last label as the first visitor’s shoes clicked on the concrete.'' Publish checklist: consistent POV, paragraphing, title, name.\n\nExplain what makes this example accurate and effective, identifying its specific wording.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Publish path.","caption":"Then clean copy.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Draft</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">events down</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">clarity pace POV</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Ending through action.","caption":"Show resolution.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Flat end</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">all fine</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Shown end</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">pins last label</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s4-eng-y7-w04-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
